-- title:
--   Theorem 6.2.3 — optimal solutions of the dual pair (6.2.1)/(6.2.2) give val(TMG) and stationary optimal policies for both players
-- statement:
--   Let $(E,A,B,p,r)$ be a two-person zero-sum stochastic game satisfying Assumption 6.2.1 (contraction) and Assumption 6.2.2 (the transition probabilities $p_{iaj}$ depend only on player I's action), and let $\beta_j > 0$, $j \in E$, be given. Let $(y^*,\rho^*)$ be an optimal solution of the linear program (6.2.1) and $(x^*,z^*)$ an optimal solution of its dual (6.2.2). Define
--   $$\pi^*_{ia} := x^*_{ia}\Big/\sum_a x^*_{ia}, \qquad a \in A(i),\ i \in E.$$
--   Then $\pi^*$ and $\rho^*$ are stationary decision rules, the stationary policies $(\pi^*)^\infty$ and $(\rho^*)^\infty$ are optimal for player I and player II respectively,
--   $$v(R_1,(\rho^*)^\infty) \le v((\pi^*)^\infty,(\rho^*)^\infty) \le v((\pi^*)^\infty,R_2) \quad \text{for all policies } R_1, R_2,$$
--   and $y^* = v((\pi^*)^\infty,(\rho^*)^\infty)$ is the value of the game.
--
--   This is the result that makes single-controller stochastic games solvable by one pair of linear programs (Algorithm XXVII), and it shows that the value and the optimal decision rules lie in the field generated by the data.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Optimality is against all history-dependent randomized policies of the opponent. The theorem is conditional on optimal solutions of both programs; the book shows (Remark 6.2.2) that they exist.
-- source:
--   Kallenberg, Linear Programming and Finite Markovian Control Problems, Mathematical Centre Tracts 148, Mathematisch Centrum, Amsterdam 1983, p. 195, Theorem 6.2.3 (with (6.2.1), (6.2.2) on p. 195; Assumptions 6.2.1, 6.2.2 on pp. 192, 194; β_j > 0 on p. 194)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_KallenbergLP_Games_SingleController

namespace KallenbergLP.Games

/-- Theorem 6.2.3 (p. 195): under Assumptions 6.2.1 and 6.2.2, with `β ≫ 0`, let `(y*, ρ*)` and
`(x*, z*)` be optimal solutions of (6.2.1) and (6.2.2), and `π*_{ia} := x*_{ia} / ∑_a x*_{ia}`.
Then `(π*)^∞` and `(ρ*)^∞` are stationary optimal policies for player I and player II, and
`y* = v((π*)^∞, (ρ*)^∞)` is the value of the game. -/
theorem lp_pair_value_optimal {N : ℕ} {α β : Type} [Fintype α] [Fintype β]
    (G : Game N α β) (hA : Assumption621 G) (hC : Assumption622 G)
    (β' : Fin N → ℝ) (hβ : ∀ j, 0 < β' j)
    (ys : Fin N → ℝ) (ρs : Fin N → β → ℝ) (h1 : LP621Optimal G β' ys ρs)
    (xs : Fin N → α → ℝ) (zs : Fin N → ℝ) (h2 : LP622Optimal G β' xs zs) :
    ∃ (hπ : IsDecisionRule1 G (piOfX G xs)) (hρ : IsDecisionRule2 G ρs),
      IsOptimalPair G (stationary1 G (piOfX G xs) hπ) (stationary2 G ρs hρ) ∧
        ∀ i, ys i = totalReward G (stationary1 G (piOfX G xs) hπ) (stationary2 G ρs hρ) i := by sorry

end KallenbergLP.Games
